   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, Phil.<div><br></div><div>I want to know what is the NA values in S-X2 item fit indices.</div><div><br></div><div>Why they occurred, and How to remedy them?</div><div><br></div><div>Seongho</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Y4kHEKYBBQAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2017-08-02T12:48:58Z",
   "subject": "Re: What is the NA values in S-X2 item fit indices?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Likely, it&#39;s because of data sparseness in the responses, resulting in negative degrees of freedom after collapsing expected cells &lt; 1. Manually collapsing polytomous items can help, but not guaranteed. </div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br clear=\"all\"><div><div class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\">Phil</span><br></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>\r\n<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
